  "This License" refers to version 3 of the GNU General Public License.

  "Copyright" also means copyright-like laws that apply to other kinds of
works, such as semiconductor masks.

  "The Program" refers to any copyrightable work licensed under this
License.  Each licensee is addressed as "you".  "Licensees" and
"recipients" may be female, male, or non-binary.

  "Modifications" means any alteration of, or addition to, a work
including translating it into another language or legal form (and
including computer programs embedded in or generated by such
modifications), or a new work based on the Program (in whole or in part).

  "Covered works" means either the unmodified Program or a work based
on the Program.

  "Propagate" a work means to do anything with it that, without
permission, would make you directly or secondarily liable for
infringement under applicable copyright law, except executing it on a
computer or modifying a private copy.  Propagation includes copying,
distribution (with or without modification), making available to the
public, and in some countries other activities.

  To "convey" a work means to propagate it in a way that enables other
parties to receive or make copies.  Mere interaction with a user through
a computer network, without transfer of a copy, is not conveying.

  An interactive user interface displays "Appropriate Legal Notices"
to the extent that it includes a convenient and prominent feature that
(1) displays an appropriate copyright notice, and (2) tells the user that
there is no warranty for the work (except to the extent that warranties
are provided), that licensees may convey the work under this License, and
how to access a copy of this License.  If the interface presents a list
of command or options, such as a menu, a prominent item in the list meets
this criterion.

  1. Source Code.

  The "source code" for a work means the preferred form of the work
for making modifications to it.  "Object code" means any non-source form of
a work.

  A "Standard Interface" means an interface that either is an official
standard defined by a recognized standards body, or, in the case of
interfaces specified for a particular programming language, one that is
widely used among developers working in that language.

  The "System Libraries" of an executable work include anything, other
than the work as a whole, that (a) is included in the normal form of
packaging a Principal Components, but which is not part of that Principal
Component, and (b) serves only to enable use of the work with that
Principal Component, or to implement a Standard Interface for which an
implementation is available to the public in source code form.  A
"Principal Component", in this instance, means a major essential
component (kernel, window system, etc.) of the specific operating
system (if any) on which the executable work runs, or a compiler used
to produce the work, or an object code interpreter used to run it.

  The "Corresponding Source" for a work in object code form means all
the source code needed to generate, compile, and produce that object
code and also all the code needed to compile and link the object code
into an executable image, as well as any associated interface definition
files, or other control information needed to verify and continue
building that work.  Corresponding Source shall not be anything that
users need to have in order to compile programs to use your executable
(for example, distribution of necessary header files and documentation).
Corresponding Source for a work in object code form means all the source
code needed to generate, compile, and produce that object code and also
all the code needed to compile and link the object code into an
executable image, as well as any associated interface definition
files, and other control information necessary to compile and link the
object code.

